1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,323
[larei] Welcome to the show
Leo, we talk about AI all the time, and there's always something new
But recently, one word keeps popping up: "Agent
"

2
00:00:09,323 --> 00:00:16,452
[leo] Agent
I feel like
isn't that just a "premium" name for a chatbot
Just rebranding it to sound more impressive

3
00:00:16,452 --> 00:00:28,746
[larei] That's actually the biggest misconception
A Chatbot "waits" for your questions and "answers" them
An Agent can "proactively" "do things" for you
It's not just a "brain"; it has "hands and feet
"

4
00:00:28,746 --> 00:00:31,579
[leo] Oh? "Proactively do things"?

5
00:00:31,579 --> 00:00:47,753
[larei] Exactly
Today, we're going to dive deep into how AI evolved from "chatting" to "executing
" Based on insights from Google, we'll explore the revolutionary changes Agents bring and a much grander future: an "Agentic Web" driven by the collaboration of countless agents

6
00:00:47,753 --> 00:00:55,658
[larei] To understand Agents, we have to look at the evolutionary path of AI
It's very clear, and it basically happened in four steps

7
00:00:55,658 --> 00:01:03,622
[larei] Step one is the basic LLM ( Large Language Model ), like early Gemini
You give it a Prompt, it gives you an answer
That's "Q&A
"

8
00:01:03,622 --> 00:01:05,620
[leo] We're all familiar with that one.

9
00:01:05,620 --> 00:01:20,156
[larei] Step two, we weren't satisfied with it only using its "known" knowledge, so RAG ( Retrieval-Augmented Generation ) emerged
The model can "retrieve" external private data or knowledge bases before answering you
Its knowledge expanded

10
00:01:20,156 --> 00:01:23,465
[leo] Right, so it can "cite its sources," so to speak

11
00:01:23,465 --> 00:01:36,200
[larei] Step three, we wanted it to not just "talk," but to "do
" This led to "Tools" calling
For example, you ask for the weather, it doesn't just guess; it actually calls a weather API, gets the data, and then tells you

12
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:38,615
[leo] So where does the Agent fit in?

13
00:01:38,615 --> 00:02:00,060
[larei] The Agent is step four
It's the fusion of all previous abilities, and it creates a fundamental change
It's no longer just calling one tool; it possesses a "Reasoning Loop
" It "thinks" for itself: "To complete this complex task, I should first call Tool A, get the result, analyze it, then call Tool B and Tool C
"

14
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:08,187
[leo] I think I get it
A Chatbot is "I'll do what you tell me to do
" An Agent is "You tell me the goal, and I'll figure out how to get it done
"

15
00:02:08,187 --> 00:02:32,813
[larei] Exactly right
This brings four revolutionary changes: First, Autonomous Action
It doesn't wait for instructions; it can proactively execute complex processes
Second, Reasoning and Planning
It thinks for itself, and can even self-correct when it hits a problem
Third, Continuous Learning
It accumulates experience during execution, getting smarter and understanding you better over time

16
00:02:32,813 --> 00:02:34,764
[leo] And the fourth one?

17
00:02:34,764 --> 00:02:38,758
[larei] The fourth, and most powerful: Multi-Agent Collaboration

18
00:02:38,758 --> 00:02:50,193
[larei] This "Multi-Agent Collaboration" is key to the future of Agents
Just like in human society, solving complex problems relies on a team of specialists, not one "jack-of-all-trades
"

19
00:02:50,193 --> 00:02:53,758
[leo] AI needs to team up
How do they even talk to each other

20
00:02:53,758 --> 00:03:03,592
[larei] This is the core problem Google is trying to solve
In a company, you might have an "Expedia Agent" for booking tickets, an "SAP Agent" for expenses, and a "Slack Agent" for approvals

21
00:03:03,592 --> 00:03:16,687
[leo] Hold on
I think I see where you're going
If I need to go on a business trip, right now I have to go to Expedia, book the flight, screenshot it, then go to SAP to fill out the expense form, and finally paste a link to my boss for approval
Right

22
00:03:16,687 --> 00:03:27,125
[larei] Exactly
The process is fragmented
But in the world of Agents, you just tell your "Root Agent": "Handle my business trip to Hangzhou next week, budget is $500
"

23
00:03:27,125 --> 00:03:28,274
[leo] And then?

24
00:03:28,274 --> 00:03:47,793
[larei] Then your "Root Agent" activates
It automatically "discovers" and "delegates" tasks: it first contacts the "Booking Agent" to secure the flight and hotel
Then, it sends the order info to the "Expense Agent" to automatically generate the expense report
Finally, it pushes the request to the "Approval Agent" to wait for your boss's click

25
00:03:47,793 --> 00:03:53,342
[leo] And I don't have to do anything
How is that possible
Are all their backends connected

26
00:03:53,342 --> 00:03:59,368
[larei] This is precisely the value of the A2A Protocol the Agent-to-Agent Protocol that Google has proposed

27
00:03:59,368 --> 00:04:15,298
[larei] The A2A Protocol is what you can think of as the "diplomatic language" or "common standard" for Agents
The Expedia Agent and the SAP Agent have completely different internal systems; they are "black boxes" to each other
But they both agree to use the A2A language for external communication

28
00:04:15,298 --> 00:04:19,141
[leo] So, A2A is like the "lingua franca" of the Agent world

29
00:04:19,141 --> 00:04:27,129
[larei] A good analogy
When Agent A wants to ask Agent B for help, it first needs to "discover" B
Agent B provides an "Agent Card
"

30
00:04:27,129 --> 00:04:31,297
[leo] A business card
What's on it
"AI Elite, Agent B"

31
00:04:31,297 --> 00:04:40,295
[larei] ( Chuckles ) Close
It's a JSON file that standardly lists: "Who I am," "What skills I have," "What input format I accept," and "What my contact address is
"

32
00:04:40,295 --> 00:04:50,151
[larei] Once A gets the card, it knows how to talk to B
It sends a standardized Message via RPC ( Remote Procedure Call )
If the task is simple, B returns the result immediately

33
00:04:50,151 --> 00:04:56,375
[leo] But "expense approval" isn't simple
The boss might not see it for hours
Does A have to just "wait online"

34
00:04:56,375 --> 00:05:11,062
[larei] Great question
This is where the "Asynchronous Priority" design of the A2A protocol comes in
For complex tasks, when B receives the request, it immediately replies to A: "Task created, ID is 123, I'm processing it
"

35
00:05:11,062 --> 00:05:13,975
[leo] So how does A know when B is finished?

36
00:05:13,975 --> 00:05:22,173
[larei] A *could* use "Polling" asking B every few minutes, "How's task 123 doing
" But that's very inefficient

37
00:05:22,173 --> 00:05:43,422
[larei] That's why A2A recommends using SSE ( Server-Side Events )
Agent A just keeps a connection open, and Agent B will "proactively" push status updates through this stream: "Task 123 submitted for approval," "Task 123 approved by boss," "Task 123 complete
" It's much more efficient

38
00:05:43,422 --> 00:05:49,029
[leo] This all sounds great, but are these Agents far-off, or does Google have any practical examples

39
00:05:49,029 --> 00:05:58,353
[leo] Ah, that one
The one where you can be interrupted mid-conversation, and it still remembers what you were talking about it maintains "In-Session Memory"

40
00:05:58,353 --> 00:06:09,509
[larei] That's the one
It can also understand the parts on your desk, help you find a tutorial on YouTube, or even find the dimensions in your email
That's a classic multi-modal, cross-application Agent

41
00:06:09,509 --> 00:06:20,307
[larei] There's another, more grounded prototype called Mariner
It's a Chrome browser plugin
Through it, the large model can "understand" the pixels, text, and forms on your screen

42
00:06:20,307 --> 00:06:22,675
[leo] Isn't that just "browser automation"?

43
00:06:22,675 --> 00:06:37,024
[larei] Yes
For example, you can give it a Google Sheet with 100 companies
Mariner will automatically open new tabs, search for each company's website, find the "Contact Us" page, and copy the email address back into the sheet

44
00:06:37,024 --> 00:06:43,944
[leo] That's a huge time-saver
And I heard that while it's working, you can pause it and actually see what it's "thinking" ( its Reasoning )

45
00:06:43,944 --> 00:06:52,211
[larei] Exactly
That kind of transparency is important
It proves Agents aren't science fiction; they are entering our workflows right now

46
00:06:52,211 --> 00:07:02,694
[larei] So we've talked about what Agents can do ( A2A collaboration ) and seen examples ( Mariner )
How do developers actually build their own
For this, Google provides a complete "Agent Stack
"

47
00:07:02,694 --> 00:07:06,038
[leo] The "full stack" again ( laughs )
What's included

48
00:07:06,038 --> 00:07:17,497
[larei] ( Laughs ) Pretty much
First, at the bottom, is the "brain": the Gemini family of models, providing reasoning
Second, is the "body" and "skeleton": the ADK ( Agent Development Kit )

49
00:07:17,497 --> 00:07:20,470
[leo] ADK? Another SDK?

50
00:07:20,470 --> 00:07:32,045
[larei] It's an open-source client SDK, and it's critical
It saves developers from worrying about the low-level details of tool calling, workflow orchestration, and session management
ADK packages all that "grunt work
"

51
00:07:32,045 --> 00:07:44,038
[larei] For instance, in ADK, you can define an Agent's persona ( Instruction ) and its Tools in just a few lines of code
You can even nest Agents, having a "Root Agent" call a "Research Agent" as a sub-task

52
00:07:44,038 --> 00:07:52,315
[leo] I get it
ADK is the "Agent construction kit," and A2A is the "communication protocol" they use *after* they're built

53
00:07:52,315 --> 00:08:05,470
[larei] Precisely
Finally, the Agent you build needs a place to "live" ( Host )
That's the Vertex AI Agent Engine
It's a fully managed cloud platform that handles scaling, security, and monitoring for you

54
00:08:05,470 --> 00:08:17,788
[leo] Larei, I have a question
Google provides the Gemini ( brain ), the ADK ( body ), and the Agent Engine ( habitat )
Are they trying to create a "monopoly"
If I use ADK, am I forced to use Gemini

55
00:08:17,788 --> 00:08:26,274
[larei] This is actually the most interesting part of Google's Agent strategy
The answer is: no
The design philosophy of ADK is "Model Agnostic
"

56
00:08:26,274 --> 00:08:28,550
[leo] Model Agnostic?

57
00:08:28,550 --> 00:08:37,884
[larei] Yes
ADK has an "LLM Core" inside
It defaults to Gemini, but it allows you to plug in any other model, whether it's closed-source or open-source

58
00:08:37,884 --> 00:08:46,767
[leo] Wow, that's a very open approach
It's like Google is providing the "car blueprints" ( ADK ), but letting you choose your own "engine" ( LLM )

59
00:08:46,767 --> 00:08:59,027
[larei] And not just that, it's also "Deployment Agnostic
" The Agent you build with ADK, once packaged as a container image, can be deployed on Google Cloud, on-premises ( Local ), or on any other cloud platform

60
00:08:59,027 --> 00:09:13,155
[leo] So Google's strategy isn't to "lock in" developers
It's to attract everyone to co-build this "Agentic Web" ecosystem by providing the best tools ( ADK ) and the most open protocol ( A2A )

61
00:09:13,155 --> 00:09:18,879
[larei] Exactly
It's betting on the openness of the ecosystem, not the exclusiveness of its platform

62
00:09:18,879 --> 00:09:32,451
[larei] So, looking back, the Agent is no longer a distant concept
It evolved from Q&A ( LLM ), to retrieval ( RAG ), to execution ( Tools ), and finally into a new species that can act, plan, and collaborate autonomously

63
00:09:32,451 --> 00:09:38,895
[leo] And the ADK and A2A protocols from Google are designed to lower the barrier to building them and allow them to "team up
"

64
00:09:38,895 --> 00:09:48,811
[larei] Finally, for all of this to happen at scale, the "brain" ( the LLM ) must be both powerful and cheap
This is why Google emphasizes the "Pareto Frontier" for its Gemini family

65
00:09:48,811 --> 00:09:50,958
[leo] "Pareto Frontier"?

66
00:09:50,958 --> 00:09:57,727
[larei] To quote Jennie Shi, it's summarized as: "The strongest is the cheapest, and the cheapest is the strongest
"

67
00:09:57,727 --> 00:10:10,139
[larei] Only when the operating cost of the Agent's "brain" is low enough, and its performance high enough, can an "Agentic Web" an internet of billions of agents collaborating to complete complex tasks for us truly be realized

68
00:10:10,139 --> 00:10:20,437
[leo] An internet where we no longer have to operate things manually, but one that understands our intent and collaborates proactively
That certainly sounds like a future worth looking forward to
Thanks for the insights today, Larei

69
00:10:20,437 --> 00:10:21,934
[larei] Thanks for listening.
